## The writing

Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text', gives the same answer for two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. Reply A is: ```json\n{\"a\": 1}\n{\"b\": 2}\n```\n{\"c\": 3}. Reply B is: {\"c\": 3}. The rule requires recover_json_object(A) to return {\"b\": 2}, because the fenced block holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {\"b\": 2}; it requires recover_json_object(B) to return {\"c\": 3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"c\": 3}. But the implementation's fenced pool tries to parse the entire fenced content as one object, fails on the two-object fence, and falls through to the top-level pool, which returns the last top-level object in the whole text, {\"c\": 3}, for both A and B. I would be wrong if recover_json_object(A) actually returned {\"b\": 2}, or if the rule were read as not counting a fence that contains more than one object as 'holding one', in which case the top-level fallback would be correct and the pair would not be a find.

